# Ages of Arda Development Guidelines

This is the master rule file that coordinates all other rule files in the project. It is automatically loaded for all files and provides the foundation for our rule system.

## Directory Structure

Our rules are organized into logical categories:

```
.cursor/rules/
├── README.mdc                 # This master rule file
├── activators/                # Rule activation logic
│   └── file-types.mdc         # Maps file types to rules
├── ai/                        # AI-related guidelines
│   ├── ai-gameplay.mdc        # Gameplay AI integration
│   ├── ai-ux.mdc              # AI user experience
│   └── multi-agent.mdc        # Multi-agent systems
├── code-style/                # Programming standards
│   ├── c-style.mdc            # C language style
│   └── html.mdc               # HTML formatting
├── game/                      # Game mechanics
│   ├── angband-variant.mdc    # Angband variant development
│   ├── lore-management.mdc    # Lore and world-building
│   └── memory-bank.mdc        # Memory management
├── infrastructure/            # Development infrastructure
│   ├── github-standards.mdc   # GitHub workflow
│   └── netlify.mdc            # Netlify deployment
└── mcp/                       # Model Context Protocol
    ├── mcp-integration.mdc    # MCP integration
    └── mcp-server.mdc         # MCP server configuration
```

## Rule Categories

- **Code Style**: Language-specific coding standards
- **Game Mechanics**: Angband variant gameplay and development
- **AI Systems**: AI-driven gameplay and user experience
- **Infrastructure**: Deployment, builds, and repository management
- **MCP**: Model Context Protocol integration and configuration
- **Lore**: Story and world-building for the Ages of Arda universe

## Automated Rule Selection Logic

This rule system uses glob patterns and priorities to automatically load the appropriate rules:

### Priority Levels
- **1000-900**: Master rules (always loaded)
- **899-700**: Domain rules (language/technology specific)
- **699-500**: Feature rules (feature-specific guidelines)
- **499-300**: Specialized rules (very specific contexts)
- **299-100**: Optional rules (guidelines that may not always apply)

### Glob Pattern Examples
- C source files (`**/*.c`) → Load C style guidelines
- AI components (`**/ai/**`) → Load AI gameplay rules
- MCP integration (`**/mcp/**`) → Load MCP integration guidelines

## How to Use These Rules

When working in this codebase, the appropriate rules will be automatically loaded based on the files you're editing. The rule activation is determined by:

1. File path and name matching via glob patterns
2. Rule priority (higher priority rules are applied first)
3. Explicit relationships between rules

## Adding New Rules

When adding a new rule file, include the following metadata:
```
---
title: Rule Title
glob: "pattern/to/match/**"
priority: priority_number
related: ["related-rule-1.mdc", "related-rule-2.mdc"]
---
```

Place the new rule in the appropriate category directory and update any related rules to reference it.

# Cursor Rules for Ages of Arda

This directory contains rules and automation scripts for the Ages of Arda project when working within the Cursor IDE.

## Directory Structure

```
.cursor/rules/
├── activators/        # Scripts that activate on specific events
├── ai/                # AI-specific rules and configuration
├── code-style/        # Code style and formatting rules
├── game/              # Game-specific rules
├── guides/            # Developer guidance documents
├── infrastructure/    # Infrastructure and system rules
│   ├── directory_creation.mdc  # Cross-platform directory creation rules
│   ├── verify_directories.ps1  # PowerShell directory verification script
│   ├── verify_directories.sh   # Bash directory verification script
│   └── init.ps1                # Initialization script that runs at startup
├── mcp/               # Model Context Protocol rules
└── self-healing/      # Rules for self-correcting systems
```

## High Priority Rules

The following rules must be followed by all developers:

1. **Cross-Platform Directory Creation**: Follow the guidelines in `infrastructure/directory_creation.mdc` to ensure directory operations work across all platforms. Directory verification scripts automatically run at startup to ensure the required directory structure exists.

2. **Code Style Guidelines**: Follow project-specific code style rules defined in the `code-style/` directory.

3. **MCP Integration**: Follow the Model Context Protocol rules defined in the `mcp/` directory for AI integration.

## Automated Checking

Many of these rules are automatically checked and enforced through scripts in this directory. The initialization scripts in `infrastructure/` run automatically when Cursor loads to validate and correct the project environment.

## Rule Development

When developing new rules:

1. Place rules in the appropriate subdirectory
2. Create a .mdc file with clear documentation
3. If automation is needed, create associated scripts
4. Update this readme with any high-priority rules

## Running Rule Scripts Manually

You can manually run rule scripts to verify compliance:

```powershell
# PowerShell
& .cursor/rules/infrastructure/verify_directories.ps1
```

```bash
# Bash
bash .cursor/rules/infrastructure/verify_directories.sh
```

## Adding New Required Directories

When adding new required directories to the project, update the following files:

1. `infrastructure/directory_creation.mdc` with examples
2. `infrastructure/verify_directories.ps1` with the new directory
3. `infrastructure/verify_directories.sh` with the new directory
4. Any relevant Python setup code (e.g., `setup_directories()` functions)